														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4842602">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes<br />
<strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article and probably experiment logs, maybe also interview logs</p>
<p><strong>Genre (Optional):</strong> Horror, Drama/Emotional</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong><br />
- The scp is an abandoned television broadcast station<br />
- The building itself is -1, the computers inside are -2<br />
- The station cannot broadcast anything but a handful of pre-existing Emergency Alert System warning messages<br />
- The computers inside can be used to choose which warning alert to broadcast, when, and on which TV channel.<br />
- Each alert message triggers a different anomalous event, the effects of which will manifest only around TV sets which are currently seeing the message. (if SCP-XXXX broadcasted to &quot;channel 3,&quot; everyone tuned to &quot;channel 3&quot; would have their program interrupted by the alert, and by the alert's anomalous effects.)</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong><br />
When a small-town local news team, low on funds, had their broadcast station destroyed by a catastrophic weather event, they had to find some way to keep broadcasting. Luckily, someone very close to the town happened to own a broadcast station, and sold it to them dirt-cheap as an act of generosity. Once the news team was set up in the brand-new station (SCP-XXXX-1), they resumed their regular schedule, and got accustomed to the new workplace without incident. However, a few weeks/months later, a storm front was rolling in, and cyclones forming close to the town. They broadcasted the tornado warning alert, triggering an anomalous disaster in their town. The connection between the warning and the anomaly was not yet apparent.<br />
They continued to use the warning messages in times that called for them, consistently triggering catastrophes in town. Eventually, the constant anomalous disasters in town devastated local businesses. With nobody paying for advertising, and the town's population facing a rapid decrease, the local news channel was no longer profitable, and was losing money. The news team fell into massive debt, and then fell apart.<br />
The Foundation took notice of the town's high rate of anomalous events, and realized the cause when the news station's shutdown corresponded with complete cessation of anomalies.<br />
<strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong><br />
The story of the town's downfall as a result of the station would be the most interesting part in my opinion.<br />
The different anomalies caused by the broadcasts could also be potentially pretty interesting.<br />
<strong>Additional Notes:</strong><br />
If it gets greenlit I'll definitely be doing a lot of research on how local news stations actually operate. If anyone has experience with local TV broadcasting, it would be good for me to have someone to consult about terminology and accuracy.</p>

							</div>

								
					
